<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the Quick Look extension may do. An app extension must be sandboxed, and the sandbox lets it
  read the one file that Quick Look hands it. It has no network entitlement, so that a preview never
  tells anyone that a file was opened, and no other than these two:

  - JavaScriptCore's compiler (allow-jit): the renderer is JavaScript, and without the compiler it
    runs 5 to 8 times slower (100 KB of Markdown in 1.3 seconds instead of 0.2), which a long
    document turns from a moment into a wait. The hardened runtime, which notarization requires,
    takes the compiler away unless asked.

  - Reading files (temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-only, of "/"): a document's images
    are files beside it, which the sandbox keeps from an extension that was handed the document
    alone (#344). Redlines is sold outside the Mac App Store, which refuses the exception, and
    notarization allows it. Read-only, and used for nothing but the images that the document
    links to: swift/PreviewImages.swift loads a file only inside the document's project folder,
    that is no hidden file's or folder's, whose name and first bytes both say it is an image, and
    that no link leads out of the project to. With no network, what it reads goes nowhere but into
    the page that Quick Look draws. scripts/build-quicklook reads the signature back, and holds it
    to exactly these.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-only</key>
  <array>
    <string>/</string>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
